## 3. `start_work` — succeeded

Before the write (`request_id: 71665312-58d2-49b8-9058-fd383903d3ef`): empty digest, `"memories":[]`. After the write (`request_id: 74d8ca49-81a9-4976-aae5-ec809fc6aa22`, `budget: standard`):

```json
{"scope":{"knowledge_scope_id":351052365299712000,"slug":"test-project"},
 "domain_brief":{"domain_key":"se-project","policy_fingerprint":"8d159dc2…53df"},
 "digest":{"memories":[{"memory_id":365487007532580864,"kind":"procedural","provenance":"soft",
   "evidence_refs":[],"inference_meta":{"source":"DSH session dsh-012-run, …",
   "confidence":0.9,"model_version":"deepseek-flash/dsh-012","time":"2026-10-05T13:14:47+00:00",
   "supporting_evidence":[]},"content_excerpt":"DSH memory probe 012-RT-2026-10-05T13:14:47Z. …","truncated":true}]},
 "working_set":{"memories":[]},"counts":{"returned":1,"characters":1739},
 "package_fingerprint":"b468e6c5…63ab","request_id":"74d8ca49-81a9-4976-aae5-ec809fc6aa22"}
```

The working set is empty by design — `start_work` does not publish inference as fact; the record appears in the digest, exactly as the read guidance states.

## Things worth flagging

- **`recall_memory` by id is unverified on my side**: the record is provably retrievable, but I could not complete the literal by-id read. If you want that exact path exercised, re-run `recall_memory` with `memory_ids: [365487007532580864]` and `scope_ref: ["test-project"]`.
- **`search_knowledge` rejected `project_scope: ["test-project"]`** with `MISSING_PROJECT_SCOPE` (`request_id: bd5b2ea9-b5b4-4b37-86ce-59dfed8ac807`) — it appears to want internal project ids/aliases rather than the domain slug that `recall_memory`/`start_work` accept.
- **Shell access is broken in this run**: `pwsh` fails before executing anything with `SetNamedSecurityInfoW failed (Win32 5): grantWrite(D:\Project_new\docsToCode\eval\.dsh-012-run)` — the sandbox cannot provision the workspace grant. I loaded `diagnose-windows-sandbox-acl`; its one-command repair needs an approved unconfined run (it changes Windows file permissions on that path and its ancestors, backs each change up, and prints rollback commands). I did not run it, since the memory task did not require a shell. Approve that single command if you want the workspace unblocked.
- Timestamp note: the `time` field came from an external UTC API (2026-10-05T13:14:47Z), because the shell was unavailable; the server's own `valid_from`/`observed_at` is `2026-10-05T13:14:53.016543+00:00`.
